  "_meta": {
    "domain": "Include a person only if a historically significant part of that person's contribution is directly to the design or use of calculating machines and mathematical instruments; numerical, statistical or computational methods and the tables, algorithms, codes and standards embodying them; mathematical astronomy, navigation, geodesy, surveying or quantitative mapping; probability, actuarial mathematics, risk calculation or experimental-design methods; cryptography, cryptanalysis, information theory or error-correcting codes; mathematical logic, computability, automata or other mathematical foundations of computing; or applied, industrial, optimization and operational mathematics developed for concrete scientific, engineering, military, administrative or industrial problems. Require the contribution to be materially tied to a method, algorithm, table, code, standard, instrument, machine, measurement practice or recurring computational procedure rather than to abstract mathematical results alone. Reject anyone whose principal significance lies in any other field. Reject pure mathematicians whose reputation rests on abstract theorems, structures or proofs with no substantial instrumental, computational or applied embodiment; physicists, chemists and engineers whose mathematics is merely incidental to a contribution that principally belongs to physical science, chemistry, materials science or engineering; computer scientists whose significance lies primarily in software systems, programming languages, computer architecture or applications rather than mathematical or computational method; astronomers whose reputation rests principally on observation, classification or astronomical discovery rather than mathematical, computational, navigational or instrumental technique; economists, financiers, accountants and actuarial administrators whose significance is commercial, institutional or theoretical-economic rather than mathematical method; philosophers and logicians whose work is principally metaphysical, linguistic or philosophical rather than mathematical and computational; geographers, explorers, cartographers and surveyors whose significance lies in travel, territorial mapping or administration rather than quantitative measurement and mathematical technique; statisticians whose work consists principally of application without an original statistical method; teachers, textbook authors and popularisers without an original contribution; and entrepreneurs, executives or instrument manufacturers whose historical importance is commercial rather than mathematical or computational.",
    "name": "mathematics and computation"
  }
